Sprint #112 — Beta-Gate A, SEC-Front (abuse hardening). The session took FEAT-28, the
layer-2 per-tenant/per-token write-rate limiter, from ratified design to a delivered,
correctly-placed feature — across two Code sub-sprints — and closed the CE/EE boundary
question the first build exposed.

**SPRINT_112.1 — build.** The limiter shipped on both write surfaces: a REST filter
(429 + Retry-After) and an MCP interceptor (isError tool-result with a speaking retry
text, not a bare JSON-RPC error), enforced post-OIDC / pre-tenant-resolution. Bucket4j
8.19.0 in-memory behind a store-adapter port; bucket 2a per token-sub enforcing, 2b
per-tenant-aggregate prepared but inert. A fail-loud scale-gate refuses boot on
multi-instance+in-memory or store=shared (the silent-scale-open trap made structurally
impossible). Degradation-fail-open. tenant_limits table (V19) with the RLS shape decided
below; internal GET/PATCH config endpoint in the D-OPS-28 pattern; ops-console Connector-tab
config surface (config-only, no live usage — audit-no-activity-monitoring upheld, verified
by an assert-by-absence structural log check). CE default band 600 burst / 120 per 60s,
sized to the fastest legit agent cadence, not typing. Both PRs green incl. Sonar; a
wire-level MCP IT (real Keycloak token, full Streamable-HTTP) proved the enforcement bites
on the real dispatch path.

**SPRINT_112.2 — CE→EE move (Variant 1).** The build was correct but sat in the OSS/CE
core; abuse-control's purpose (co-tenant availability, threat-model G1) is a multi-tenant
property the CE does not have. The limiter was relocated into a new ee-server module
`abuse-control` (package ai.kumbuka.saas.ratelimit), composed via tenant-resolution —
**OSS-footprint zero**. Stage-0 localization refuted Amendment 2's "priority-registered"
premise (both enforcement points were method annotations dying with the unmerged #85);
the realized zero-footprint mechanism is a global JAX-RS ResourceInfo filter (REST) + an
interceptor bound to the class-wide @TenantBound narrowed to memory_remember (MCP), both
with fail-loud reflective seam-resolution that breaks the build/ITs on any future
kumbuka-server rename/removal — never a silent disarm. The migration is EE-isolated
(V19 → V10000 under db/ee-migration). CE-absence proven (kumbuka-server main tops at V18;
#85 closed unmerged + branch deleted — the limiter never entered CE-main history).
ops-console #135 needed no repoint (env-driven proxy → the EE image).

**Decisions (persisted this session).** D-OPS-35 Amendment 2, Part A (CE/EE placement,
Variant 1) + Part B (tenant_limits RLS = read-open FOR SELECT USING(true) as a named,
bounded tenant-isolation exception, Option A — writes tenant-bound, no query path, prod
BYPASSRLS so no live hole, EE-only; rejected SECURITY-DEFINER as disproportionate), plus an
as-built correction recording the actual enforcement mechanism and the deliberate,
fail-loud-guarded EE→OSS seam coupling. Two follow-on ratifications: the EE-Flyway
convention (db/ee-migration, V10000+ namespace — mnemonic convention.ee-flyway-v10000-namespace)
and the single-source kumbuka-server.version pin. Ledger + INDEX + threat-model §10 + two
mnemonics updated.

**Teardown complete.** ee-server #15 merged, ops-console #135 merged, server #85 closed
unmerged (public-safe comment), its feature branch deleted. FEAT-28 lives exclusively in EE.

**Deferred (carry-forward).** The deploy wave (new EE image + 3 env-vars — the limiter runs
on defaults meanwhile, limits endpoint 503 until the token is set); the ee-server Sonar gate
(P1 — the finding below); the single-source pin (P3); Q4 store-down alert and the
purge-tenant tenant_limits restrow (both P3, real only later). FEAT-29 quota is the next
abuse layer and the first consumer of the V10000 convention.
