       $kbimp$# Sprint 134 — The logbook specification, and the order the steering layer runs on

## What this sprint was for

The logbook is the second steering service. Before it could be built, the object it transports had to
exist as a ratified contract rather than as a habit. This sprint produced that contract, measured the
corpus the order runs against, and dispatched the last worklist-manager work in the same pass.

Seven decisions were ratified. Two of them correct decisions taken earlier in the same week.

## Ratified decisions

All persisted in `spec/docs/decisions/kumbuka-decisions-steering-services.md`, index updated.

**D-STEER-1 Amendment 1 — the partition was a description, not a mechanism.** D-STEER-1 claimed each
service "cannot stage what it does not own". The mechanism behind that claim was a closed exclusion
list, which protects an open set: everything unnamed — `handover/`, `analysis/`, `TECH-DEBT.md`,
`SPRINT-CHRONICLE.md` — landed in both work trees. Replaced by per-service inclusion roots.
`handover/` becomes `dispatches/` and is logbook-owned.

**D-STEER-4 — the logbook owns intent, the worklist owns state.** A dispatch may instruct a state
change; it may not assert one. Generalised: no logbook object carries a run-time-state claim of any
kind. The previous prohibition covered only frozen git tokens; this covers every ageing claim, because
under a queue model a briefing may wait days between writing and reading.

**D-STEER-5 — one dispatch object class, three roles, frozen at send.** The session briefing becomes a
`dispatch` with `apparatus: concept`; the sprint record becomes its `return`. `draft → open` is the
send and freezes the object. Eleven normative fields. Mandatory prose: Auftrag, Frame, Grenze, plus
Regime for code and design.

**D-STEER-6 — ordering.** `active` leaves the sort key; claims express work in progress. Key becomes
`(not-P1, cluster rank, priority rank, size, Nr)` with P1 absolute. `next` reports its band. Selection
stays an operator ratification act; order is mechanical.

**D-STEER-2 Amendment 1 — the git transport belongs to the core.** Decided on the fail-loud returned
by SPRINT_133.1. `SteeringRepo` moves into the core; `gitio` and `SteeringRepo` collapse into one git
discipline. Acceptance criterion mechanical: `test_core_isolation.py` passes unmodified.

**D-STEER-7 — both steering services live in one repository over one shared core.** The logbook is
built inside `Kumbuka-ai/worklist-manager` as a second service layer. Two images, two matrix rows,
decoupled release cadences.

**D-STEER-6 Amendment 1 — `next` proposes only ready work.** Ratifies the reading SPRINT_134.1 flagged
rather than assumed. Consequence stated in the ledger: with 43 of 45 open feature rows on `M?`, `next`
structurally proposes no feature at all, which makes CHORE-232 blocking.

## U1 — the corpus, measured

Measured against the running service, 230 open rows.

| Axis | Distribution |
|---|---|
| Status | 19 `active` · 206 `todo` · 5 `new` |
| Priority | 7 P1 · 49 P2 · 124 P3 · 2 M1 · 43 `M?` · 5 none |
| Cluster | HK 70 · DEPLOY 50 · CORE 36 · SEC 35 · GTM 13 · CONN 9 · EE 9 · BETA 3 |
| Size within P3 | S 78 · M 44 · L 2 |

**One prediction on record was refuted.** The target-architecture analysis expected P2 to hold the
majority of open non-feature rows. P3 holds 124 against 49. P3 is not a priority level, it is the
default value. Three predictions held: P1 single-digit (7), at least forty `M?` feature rows (43), one
cluster above a quarter (HK at 30 %).

Three structural findings drove D-STEER-6:

- `next` returned all 19 `active` rows before any `todo` row. Positions seven and eight were P3 rows
  from CONN; a P1 security defect on `todo` sat at position twenty. Status outranked priority.
- Within P3, 78 rows shared size S, so their effective order came from `Nr` — an artefact of the order
  three source files were concatenated in during the schema migration.
- The 43 `M?` rows sorted behind everything and `next` did not say so.

**The largest cluster is the steering layer itself.** HK holds more than CORE and SEC combined. An
abort rule for the steering-layer investment was raised as an open question and not decided.

## Dispatch

**SPRINT_134.1** — worklist manager: the push path and the ordering. First object written under the
D-STEER-5 contract: no state assertions, no fixed sections, `draft` then flipped to `open` as the send.

Returned green. Both ratified decisions implemented in one pass; all six Stage-0 predictions confirmed
with one refinement (cluster did not appear in the old sort key at all); every red probe observed
failing before passing; `test_core_isolation.py` green unmodified; PR #16 merged by the operator,
Sonar quality gate OK at 94.2 % new-code coverage. One design judgement returned for ratification
instead of being decided — see D-STEER-6 Amendment 1.

CHORE-289 and CHORE-291 booked `done`. CHORE-292 (inclusion roots) registered and deliberately bundled
with the logbook build.

## Keycloak — the logbook auth surface

DNS for `log.jbaconsult.com` in place. Clients `log-ui` (confidential) and `log-mcp` (public, PKCE
S256) created live in the `kumbuka-ops` realm, both with the `kumbuka-ops` audience mapper, `log-mcp`
additionally with the realm-roles mapper. The `log-ui` secret was rotated after it appeared in a
transcript.

**Measured, and it changes how this repository must be treated:** `--import-realm` runs on first boot
only. The realm template is a bootstrap, not a write path. Live realm and template are two truths that
drift silently, and the template only wins once — on a fresh deploy. Both were therefore updated:
client definitions in the template, `LOG_UI_CLIENT_SECRET` in the `entrypoint.sh` allow-list and in
`.env.example`, and the README now states the bootstrap semantics.

Two deliberate deviations from the `wlm` template: `log-ui` carries only the audience mapper (the
template gives `wlm-ui` a redundant realm-roles mapper the live client does not have), and no
`post.logout.redirect.uris` (the live client has none — template and realm agree).

## Findings

**F — a check that cannot distinguish the positive case is not a check.** Four times this session an
assertion was built that would have reported failure regardless of the underlying state: a PKCE probe
reading `--fields attributes` (which the list endpoint does not return), a rotation probe reading a
command's own silent output, a `grep -c || echo 0` construction emitting two lines and breaking the
integer comparison, and a `python3 -m json.tool` run treated as evidence that inserted content had
landed. The last produced a false GO. Instruments must be calibrated against a known positive before
being armed.

**F — a read-back through the same connector does not verify a write.** Four edits to the `keycloak`
submodule appeared to vanish: the connector returned success diffs and read them back, while a content
grep on disk found nothing and `gitstatus` reported the submodule clean. The conclusion drawn at the
time — that the writes had been silently reverted — was wrong. The submodule was on a feature branch
that already carried the change as a commit, later merged to its `main` as `e920edf`; the working tree
was clean because the work was *done*, not because it was lost. The measurement that would have settled
it in one step — which branch the submodule was on — was never taken. The underlying rule still holds
and is the reason this was confusing at all: a connector read-back only establishes that the connector
agrees with itself. Verification needs an external check, and for a submodule that check must include
the branch, not only the file content.

**F — `set -e` in an interactively pasted block kills the SSH session.** The flag applies to the login
shell, so the first failing command ends the session rather than a script. Observed once with session
loss and an unknown completion point. Runbook blocks run in a subshell or without `set -e`, and they
must be idempotent, because a partially completed block is the normal case.

**F — kcadm surface facts.** `get clients --fields attributes` returns an empty object; verification
requires the full representation. The `client-secret` endpoint knows `get` and `create` but not
`update`; `create` rotates and prints nothing, so success must be established by a separate `get`.
The stored note claiming `kcadm create` silently drops the `attributes` block is now under suspicion —
measured here, `create -s` sets attributes correctly and it was the *reading* that failed silently.

**Corrected in-session, worth recording:** three claims were made from missing measurements rather than
from evidence — a failed `tool_search` read as a dead service, an unfetched remote ref read as a failed
push, an empty field projection read as missing PKCE enforcement. All three were withdrawn. The
pattern is the same as the finding above, seen from the other side.

## Anchor edit hazard

An INDEX edit anchored on the closing words of the clause being replaced swallowed that clause and
attached new content under the wrong decision ID. Repaired in the same turn. Rule: anchor on text that
is not itself being replaced.

## Open at close

- Transactionality of a partial closure is accepted practice but not a ratified clause in D-STEER-4.
- Skill-drift guard for `session-start` (D-STEER-5 consequence 3) has no corpus row.
- 19 rows on `active` need disposition; 43 `M?` markers need CHORE-232. Neither is ordering work.
- Infra strand for the logbook: matrix row, compose service, Caddy snippet, `LOG_VERSION` seeded in
  `deploy.env` before the first deploy.
- Keycloak template change is committed and merged (`e920edf` on the submodule's `main`). Nothing open.
- Abort rule for the steering-layer investment share.
