$kbimp$, $kbimp$concept$kbimp$, DATE '2026-08-07', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-08-07T00:00:00Z',
       $kbimp$# Sprint 135 — the logbook object model, and the service that carries it

Date: 2026-08-07 · Superrepo `cbb9f82` · spec `d6a680c` · worklist-manager `8284fbb`

## What this sprint was for, and what it turned out to be

The assignment was CHORE-269: build the logbook. The STARTER declared the specification complete.

It was not. Walking the axes one at a time surfaced two contradictions inside the ratified text, one
class that had been explicitly left undefined while carrying the weight of another decision, and seven
areas that had never been discussed at all — the MCP verb surface beyond a single query, searchability,
the web interface, finding categorisation, the import of the existing corpus, serialisation, and the
transport of a dispatch to the code apparatus, which the task title named and no decision solved.

So the sprint became a specification sprint that then also shipped three quarters of the build. Both
halves landed.

## Decisions

**D-STEER-8** consolidates the logbook object model and replaces the D-STEER-5 field table. Two roles
instead of three; identity is `id` plus role, which removes `counterpart` entirely and closes the
contradiction between a send-freeze and a field required only after the counterpart exists. A correction
is a new dispatch carrying `extends`, forming a star and never a chain. The return is optional and the
pair invariant conditional. There is no `update` verb, so the freeze is a property of the surface rather
than a check inside it. Storage runs by sprint, filenames are mechanical, the web interface is read-only,
and the code apparatus reaches the service over the same public client.

**D-STEER-9** defines findings, which D-STEER-4 had deliberately left open. A finding is an observation
ledger whose value is repetition; the number arrives only on survival of the sprint, allocated through
the session rather than across a service boundary; the row is thin and outside the sort key; the document
carries no status and is append-only in `occurrences`.

**D-STEER-1 Amendment 2** reverses the logbook branch clause to direct-to-`main`. Reading it closely
showed the branch rested on a permission argument — files cannot collide — and never on a purpose, while
D-STEER-9's append-only findings destroyed the premise anyway.

Four amendments followed within the same session: D-STEER-8 Am. 1 (sprints unchained, start conditions,
the checking-only close verb, `curated-in` as an object pointer, design without logbook access,
validation in the shared core), D-STEER-9 Am. 1 (slug grammar and search with a normative return shape,
`related` opened to three anchor kinds), D-STEER-9 Am. 2 (`occurrences` as flat strings, forced by the
stdlib-only core), and D-STEER-8 Am. 2 (claim-freedom out of the close verb, `extends` inside its sprint,
a correction may form its own pair, the start path activating only when unambiguous plus the `activate`
verb that gap opens, and a red probe to replace the suppressed Sonar watcher).

The most consequential single change is that **sprints are no longer chained**. A sprint opens by writing
its concept dispatch at any time; arbitrarily many may be open; the number is identity, not order.

## Sub-sprints

`SPRINT_135.1` — core package renamed `worklist` → `steering`, isolation gate re-anchored. PR #17.
The measured carrier list came out far smaller than predicted: `sonar-project.properties` and both
workflows are directory-based, and `deploy/Dockerfile` runs the service package, which is not renamed.
The gate turned out not to be what the analysis claimed — it enforces stdlib-only, not core-versus-service
— which means a future `logbook_service` import into the core is caught with no new rule at all. Proven
by a red probe rather than argued.

`SPRINT_135.2` — Stage 0 (the rename's documentation and invocation carriers, `prog="steering"`, `repo.py`
added to the presence gate) as PR #18, then Stages 1–3 (frontmatter and schema validation in the shared
core, the logbook domain and state machine, a sixteen-verb MCP surface) as PR #19. Both red probes shown
red then green; thirteen CI jobs and the blocking Sonar gate green. Stage 4 and Stage 5 remain.

`SPRINT_135.3` and `SPRINT_135.5` — the logbook viewer, design apparatus, two rounds. The first round
solved the pair as two columns under one identity and rendered a missing return as a designed state; it
left the correction star named but unsolved and rendered a corrupt pair as an orange border that said
nothing. The second round resolved all three star problems, gave the corrupt pair words instead of colour,
and returned five open questions rather than deciding them silently. Accepted; the handover lives under
`spec/design/wlm-log/handover/logbook-viewer/`.

## Findings and slugged first sightings

slug: edit-file-size-limit-reports-as-type-error
`workspace:edit_file` rejects an oversized payload with a schema type error naming the argument shape,
so the diagnosis points at quoting rather than at size. The remedy is to split and anchor every chunk on
the same previously verified anchor, inserting in reverse order.

slug: ratified-row-cannot-be-retracted
A ratified corpus row cannot be set to `dropped`: the schema requires an empty ID for that status and
`ratify` assigns one irreversibly. CHORE-278 registers the missing retraction verb but understates it —
it assumes discarding works. Recorded there as n=2. The workaround follows CHORE-123: `done` with an
OBSOLET ref.

slug: container-write-tool-accepts-workspace-path
Two write tools accept the same absolute path and write to different machines. `create_file` writes into
the container; only the workspace connector reaches the Mac. The failure is silent and the success
message indistinguishable from a real one.

slug: workspace-connector-drops-mid-session
Twice in one session: a four-minute timeout, then a crash mid-write. The concept apparatus has one write
path to disk, and when it drops, ratified decisions live only in the chat window.

**Withdrawn:** `write-path-skips-schema-validation`. The inference — create accepts what ratify rejects,
therefore the write path skips the schema — was drawn from observed behaviour without reading the schema.
The title check is status-conditional and `new` rows are exempt by design. Derivation in place of
measurement, on the day that rule was cited three times against other reasoning. No number was allocated.
The build requirement survives on logbook grounds, which never needed the sibling.

## Corpus

CHORE-292 re-pointed at D-STEER-1 Amendment 2 (two inclusion roots, `dispatches/` dropped). CHORE-300,
-301 and -302 were created and immediately retracted: steering tooling does not belong in the product
corpus, and their content travels in the dispatch instead. The rule that came out of it — what is built on
the tooling belongs in the dispatch that builds the tooling; `scope: meta *` is a label on legacy rows,
not a filing cabinet for new ones.

## Open

Stage 4 (read-only web interface, with the accepted design handover as its specification) and Stage 5
(the thin finding row and its status vocabulary in the worklist schema) are the first order of the next
sprint. Neither is deployed: the infra matrix row, compose service, image, `LOG_VERSION` seed and edge
snippet are a separate strand, and the two Keycloak clients already exist.

The import of the existing corpus is deliberately off the critical path — the logbook works empty.

`closure.py` stays until the logbook is deployed **and** one closure has run through it end to end.
Deleting it before that means rebuilding the next closure by hand, line by line through the context
window, which is where the worklist migration already broke once. And not all of it dies: the logbook
takes sprint records, findings and dispatches, but WORKLIST, REGISTRY and ARCHIVE belong to the other
service, which has no closure verbs at all. That half needs a home before the script can go — which is an
open question, not a settled one.

The console's `tsconfig.json` and `next-env.d.ts` were discarded, not because they are build artefacts but
because they carry an unreviewed toolchain change: `jsx` moved from `preserve` — the value Next requires,
since the framework wants its JSX untouched — to `react-jsx`, and the generated route types moved from
`.next/types` to `.next/dev/types`, rewriting both the `include` list and the reference style in
`next-env.d.ts`. A Prettier reformat of every array camouflaged both. It returns on every `next dev`, so
the real question is whether the pinned Next version, the lockfile and CI still agree — CHORE-303.

Three skills are structurally obsolete and were not touched: `session-start` boots over `gitstatus` and a
workspace root, `code-handover` writes a file, and `session-closure` drives a script over files that stop
existing after the move. D-STEER-8 also moved the fixed sections out of the dispatch object and into the
`session-start` skill, so the skills now carry more than before while CHORE-297, the drift guard, remains
unbuilt. For an autonomous agent a skill is an execution instruction rather than a memo, which makes this
a precondition of the agentic work path rather than housekeeping.

Concept documents are to move out of git and into Kumbuka as soon as possible, because git is reachable
from one machine only and there is no GitHub connector for the mobile app. That is D-CTX, deferred at
Sprint 106 to approximately Sprint 120 — a reactivation trigger that passed fifteen sprints ago with
nothing watching it.
