| 0039 | The steering services are reimplemented on Quarkus, not migrated | Accepted 08-14 | steering services / build · data | **Fills the language slot ADR-0038 §4 deliberately left open.** No migration and no port: the behaviour of the worklist manager and the logbook is specified in full, then both are reimplemented as **two independent Quarkus services in two independent repositories**; the running Python implementation becomes the **source of an evidence-bearing inventory**, not the subject of continued development. Grounds: the git premise (ADR-0038 §1 premise B) sits in the domain model rather than in configuration — the store module is a clone manager holding domain logic, and D-STEER-2 Am. 1 deliberately pulled the git transport into the dependency-free core, so a port would carry that shape into a SQL world **while looking like continuity**; six build items ADR-0038 requires (tenant-realm auth, platform ingress, schema+role+Flyway per service, RLS with tenant binding, the trigger-and-GRANT split of the three storage regimes, the token as a column) are proven in the Quarkus stack and first-of-their-kind in the present one; one language means one guard, CI and release regime instead of two. **The specification gate is part of the decision:** no implementation code before the specification is accepted against a **countable** completeness measure — the risk of this milestone is not the language but the ~350 core + 263 service tests that encode behaviour learned by *observed failure* (kind mandatory with no default because a default reaches silently for the wrong object; `M0`/`Mx` as objectless markers; the finding collision guard; warnings at exit code zero), none of it in ADR-0038 and much of it only in test code. The measure itself is decided separately, before the specification is dispatched; what is fixed is that one must exist, else the clause is an assurance without a mechanism (`convention.no-gate-without-red-probe`). **UI explicitly out of scope** — the two read-only viewers are not ported; the single console is designed anew under M6 (ADR-0038 §10, ADR-0033), and during the transition the MCP verb surface is the only reading window onto a new-store corpus. **Two repositories** supersede D-STEER-7 Am. 1's one-image-two-version-keys, whose purpose (rollout decoupling) is achieved by construction once the build contexts separate. **The emergency command line lapses without replacement** — ADR-0038 open question 4 resolved via its *first* exit, on the ground that its **purpose** falls with the git store (the state files it repaired are git artifacts; the corpus table ceases to exist per §14) and that the emergency tool for a database is `psql`; an explicit repeal, not a silent step-over. Rejected: keep Python and move only the store (a rewrite wearing the appearance of continuity) · merge the two services (ADR-0038 §4, measured verb count) · fold into `kumbuka-server` (§4 holds three containers, §14 holds release cadences independent) · one repository for both (reproduces the coupling D-STEER-7 Am. 1 had to work around) · port the viewers (preserves an IA that M6 replaces). Partially supersedes **D-STEER-2 (+Am. 1)** and **D-STEER-7 Am. 1** by named clause (§6) · revises **ADR-0038 §4** · realises **CHORE-315** |

