Block 3, eine einzelne Tabellenzeile fuer docs/INDEX-adr.md, nach der 0039-Zeile:

| 0040 | Scope is the leading discriminator: the tenancy model of the steering services | Accepted 08-15 | steering services / data · core | Supplies the tenancy model **ADR-0038 and ADR-0039 both presuppose and neither states** (measured: the worklist manager takes NO corpus argument, the logbook takes one as a directory selection inside a clone rather than an isolation boundary, and the three north-star invariants have no counterpart in either). **Scope is the unit of isolation AND of content grouping; the tenant is reached THROUGH it** — one boundary, not a pair, RLS discriminating on scope; this makes ADR-0038 §10 a property of the model rather than a requirement on the interface. **NO global scope for worklist and logbook** (the memory engine keeps one): an unpartitioned everything-list would invite exactly the partitioning scopes already give away free; the resulting console asymmetry is M6's to explain, and a view that does not explain it reads as a defect. **Numbers unique per SCOPE, not per tenant** — `F-0011` in scope A and B are different objects, full address `(scope, class, number)`, so the present corpus becomes scope one and every existing identifier keeps its meaning: no renumbering. **NO hard delete** — retraction is a status, which makes the row maximum a high-water mark BY CONSTRUCTION (no second allocation counter, no reissued number, no old reference silently repointing); a DOMAIN rule, not a store rule, so tenant deletion still removes everything. **Number classes tenant-defined, object KINDS product-defined** — a freely definable kind would mean freely definable invariants, i.e. configuration instead of invariants; vocabularies become tenant DATA (retargeting CHORE-313). **THREE mechanisms, not one successor to git**: intent in the logbook · versioning of mutable rows as an ENTERPRISE capability with the rationale field where the commit message sat (the logbook needs none — it freezes at `send`, so a frozen object is its own version; CE worklist therefore keeps NO history) · **audit as a PLATFORM capability, not per service**, because three logs with three formats answer the only question ever asked — who touched *my* data — three times by half; consequence: **no `logbook-ee`**, two services yield THREE repositories not four. **Cross-scope references named by URI, never resolved by the store** — `log://<scope>/finding/0011`, service in the SCHEME (product-defined, closed set), class in the PATH (tenant-defined per §2.5, so a scheme-per-class would push tenant data into the grammar); the scope implies the tenant without naming it, so a reference never discloses whose it is; resolution belongs to session or console, viability resting on exactly two properties — numbers not reused and scope ids globally unique. ACCEPTED RISK: **no audit record between cutover and M4, in any edition** (the steering services never needed one — one operator, one corpus, and a commit history recording every write as a side effect; M5 removes all three at once), which bounds the operator-access claim accordingly (→ D-GTM-12). Rejected: tenant as leading discriminator (two boundaries where one suffices) · a global scope mirroring the memory engine · numbers per tenant (would force renumbering) · a separate allocation counter (second source of truth) · one audit log per service (answers correlation not at all) · a URI scheme per class · resolution inside the storing services. Deliberately BRIEF against this table's density (CHORE-316). Refines **ADR-0038 §3/§5/§10** and **ADR-0039** · realises **CHORE-315** · decomposed into FEAT-74…79 and CHORE-317…320 |
